# Language Grammar (BNF)

The complete grammar of the source language, in Backus-Naur Form. The
grammar below matches the implemented recursive-descent parser exactly:
each non-terminal corresponds to one parse function.

Notation: `<x>` is a non-terminal, `"x"` is a literal token, `|` separates
alternatives, and `ε` is the empty production.

---

## 1. Program and statements

```bnf
<program>          ::= <statement_list>

<statement_list>   ::= <statement> <statement_list>
                     | ε

<statement>        ::= <decl_stmt>
                     | <assign_stmt>
                     | <if_stmt>
                     | <while_stmt>
                     | <for_stmt>
                     | <print_stmt>

<block>            ::= "{" <statement_list> "}"
```

### Declaration and assignment

```bnf
<decl_stmt>        ::= <type> <identifier> "=" <expression> ";"

<type>             ::= "পূর্ণসংখ্যা"
                     | "দশমিকসংখ্যা"
                     | "লেখা"
                     | "বুলিয়ান"

<assign_stmt>      ::= <identifier> "=" <expression> ";"
```

### Control flow

```bnf
<if_stmt>          ::= "যদি" "(" <expression> ")" <block>
                     | "যদি" "(" <expression> ")" <block> "নাহয়" <block>

<while_stmt>       ::= "যতক্ষণ" "(" <expression> ")" <block>

<for_stmt>         ::= "প্রতি" "(" <identifier> "=" <expression>
                             "থেকে" <expression> ")" <block>
                     | "প্রতি" "(" <identifier> "=" <expression>
                             "থেকে" <expression>
                             "ধাপ" <expression> ")" <block>
```

### Output

```bnf
<print_stmt>       ::= "দেখাও" "(" <expression> ")" ";"
```

---

## 2. Expressions

Each level of the expression grammar is one precedence level. The
left-recursive productions encode left associativity: `ক - খ - গ` parses
as `(ক - খ) - গ`.

```bnf
<expression>       ::= <logical_or>

<logical_or>       ::= <logical_or> "||" <logical_and>
                     | <logical_and>

<logical_and>      ::= <logical_and> "&&" <equality>
                     | <equality>

<equality>         ::= <equality> <equality_op> <relational>
                     | <relational>

<relational>       ::= <relational> <relational_op> <additive>
                     | <additive>

<additive>         ::= <additive> <additive_op> <multiplicative>
                     | <multiplicative>

<multiplicative>   ::= <multiplicative> <multiplicative_op> <unary>
                     | <unary>

<unary>            ::= "!" <unary>
                     | "-" <unary>
                     | <primary>

<primary>          ::= <int_literal>
                     | <decimal_literal>
                     | <string_literal>
                     | <bool_literal>
                     | <identifier>
                     | "(" <expression> ")"
```

### Operators

```bnf
<equality_op>       ::= "==" | "!="
<relational_op>     ::= "<" | ">" | "<=" | ">="
<additive_op>       ::= "+" | "-"
<multiplicative_op> ::= "*" | "/"
```

### Precedence summary

From lowest to highest binding:

| Level | Operators | Associativity |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `\|\|` | left |
| 2 | `&&` | left |
| 3 | `==` `!=` | left |
| 4 | `<` `>` `<=` `>=` | left |
| 5 | `+` `-` | left |
| 6 | `*` `/` | left |
| 7 | `!` `-` (unary) | right |
| 8 | `( )` literals, identifiers | — |

---

## 3. Lexical grammar

These productions describe the tokens the lexer produces. Whitespace and
comments separate tokens and are otherwise discarded.

```bnf
<bool_literal>     ::= "সত্যি" | "মিথ্যা"

<int_literal>      ::= <digit_seq>

<decimal_literal>  ::= <digit_seq> "." <digit_seq>
                     | <digit_seq> "."

<digit_seq>        ::= <bangla_digit>
                     | <bangla_digit> <digit_seq>

<bangla_digit>     ::= "০" | "১" | "২" | "৩" | "৪"
                     | "৫" | "৬" | "৭" | "৮" | "৯"
```

### Identifiers

Identifiers are Bangla-only by design. An identifier begins with a Bangla
letter and may then contain Bangla letters, Bangla vowel signs and other
combining marks, Bangla digits, and the underscore.

```bnf
<identifier>       ::= <bangla_letter> <ident_tail>

<ident_tail>       ::= <ident_char> <ident_tail>
                     | ε

<ident_char>       ::= <bangla_letter>
                     | <bangla_mark>
                     | <bangla_digit>
                     | "_"
```

`<bangla_letter>` is any Unicode codepoint in the Bengali block that is a
consonant or an independent vowel:

```
U+0985–U+098C, U+098F–U+0990, U+0993–U+09A8, U+09AA–U+09B0,
U+09B2, U+09B6–U+09B9, U+09BD, U+09CE, U+09DC–U+09DD,
U+09DF–U+09E1, U+09F0–U+09F1
```

`<bangla_mark>` is any vowel sign (কার), hasant, candrabindu, anusvara,
visarga or nukta:

```
U+0981–U+0983, U+09BC, U+09BE–U+09C4, U+09C7–U+09C8,
U+09CB–U+09CD, U+09D7, U+09E2–U+09E3
```

### Text literals

```bnf
<string_literal>   ::= '"' <text_char_list> '"'

<text_char_list>   ::= <text_char> <text_char_list>
                     | ε

<text_char>        ::= <any_character_except_quote_backslash_or_newline>
                     | <escape>

<escape>           ::= "\n" | "\t" | "\" '"' | "\\"
```

### Comments and whitespace

```bnf
<comment>          ::= "#" <any_characters_to_end_of_line>

<whitespace>       ::= " " | "\t" | "\r" | "\n"
```

---

## 4. Notes on the grammar

**Dangling else.** `<if_stmt>` has an optional `নাহয়` branch. Because both
branches of `যদি` are `<block>` rather than a bare statement, an `নাহয়`
can only attach to the `যদি` whose block it follows, so the classic
dangling-else ambiguity does not arise.

**Mandatory braces.** Every body is a `<block>`, so `যদি (…) দেখাও(ক);`
without braces is a syntax error. This removes a second source of
ambiguity and keeps the grammar unambiguous throughout.

**Statement terminators.** Declarations, assignments and print statements
end in `;`. Control-flow statements end in `}` and take no semicolon.

**The `প্রতি` range.** The ending value is exclusive, matching the
generated `range()` call: `প্রতি (গণনা = ০ থেকে ৫)` runs five times, with
গণনা taking ০, ১, ২, ৩, ৪. The `ধাপ` clause may be negative for a
countdown.

**Left recursion.** The expression rules are written left-recursively to
document associativity. A recursive-descent parser cannot execute left
recursion directly, so the implementation uses the standard equivalent
loop — parse one operand, then repeatedly consume an operator and another
operand — which produces the same left-associated tree.

---

## 5. Rules enforced outside the grammar

The following are context-sensitive and so are checked by the type
checker rather than expressed in the grammar:

- A variable must be declared before it is used, and may not be
  redeclared in the same scope.
- The condition of `যদি` and `যতক্ষণ` must be `বুলিয়ান`.
- Arithmetic operators require numeric operands. `+` also joins two
  `লেখা` values; mixing `লেখা` with a number is an error.
- Division always produces `দশমিকসংখ্যা`, even for two integers.
- An assignment must match the declared type, except that a
  `পূর্ণসংখ্যা` value may widen into a `দশমিকসংখ্যা` variable.
- The `প্রতি` bounds and `ধাপ` must be `পূর্ণসংখ্যা`; a literal `ধাপ ০`
  is rejected; the loop variable must not reuse an existing name.